  "text": "It is well known that light waves that are almost perfectly single‑frequency, such as those from two radio transmitters tuned to the same frequency, can produce a pattern of bright and dark regions when they overlap. In a quantum view, the light particles emitted by each transmitter can be represented by wave functions in a quantum configuration space. If the particles from the two sources are treated as distinguishable, the overall wave function is simply the product of the two individual ones, and no overlapping pattern appears. The situation changes when the overall wave function is forced to be either symmetric or antisymmetric, corresponding to the two possible statistical behaviors of identical particles. Assuming each source emits a fixed number of light particles, the relative phases become random, so even after symmetrizing the wave function the average particle density matches the classical prediction and no interference is seen. To observe an interference effect under these conditions one must look for a phenomenon whose classical description does not rely on phase relationships, which will be explored later in the paper."
